ntdll, server: anonymous timers are kept for reuse; timer counters for FEX.

With patch 0009 in (b157, Proton d2r8) D2R's protection thread (b157 tid 596) makes an
unnamed timer, sets it, waits for it and closes it about 93 times a second in play: a
create_timer, set_timer, get_inproc_sync_fd and close_handle request each time
(create_timer and set_timer both at a median of 93/s, cancel_timer 0).

As 0008 does for events, NtClose now keeps such a timer open in a small pool (16 per type)
when nothing can still happen to it: made by NtCreateTimer with an access that can set and
wait (TIMER_MODIFY_STATE and SYNCHRONIZE) and no name, root, security descriptor or other
attributes than OBJ_CASE_INSENSITIVE and OBJ_OPENIF; closed by the thread that made it
within 100 ms; never set with an APC or a period, never set again before its last setting
fired, every setting seen to fire by a wait of that thread; never cancelled, duplicated or
given new handle flags, and never set, waited on or queried by another thread. Then no
timeout or APC is pending in the server, a synchronization timer's signal was taken by that
wait and a notification timer's is still set. NtCreateTimer of the same type and access
takes a kept timer back: no create_timer, no close_handle, and the inproc sync fd stays
cached, so no get_inproc_sync_fd either. Three of the four requests per cycle go.

The server's timer still holds the old due time and signaled flag (Proton 11 never clears a
synchronization timer's flag once it fired). So the first NtSetTimer of a reused timer
carries a marker in the argument of the request (unused without an APC) and the server
first puts the timer back to the state create_timer gives (no timeout or APC, not signaled,
sync reset, due time 0, period 0), then sets it, in the same request. Anything that could
see the old state before that (a wait on a notification timer that fired, NtCancelTimer,
NtQueryTimer, a setting with an APC, duplication, new handle flags, any use by another
thread) sends the marker in a request of its own first. The marker carries a reuse number
and the server acts on the first marker of each reuse only, so a thread that looks at the
timer while the first setting is on its way still finds a new timer, and that setting is
never undone. A program's own argument that looks like the marker (without APC) is sent as
NULL; the server never reads it then.

For FEX, appended to __wine_d2r_server_calls_ext at byte 2112 (8 x ULONG64, to byte 2175):
tm_kept, tm_reused, tm_reset (marker in a request of its own), tm_armed (closed before its
setting was seen to fire), tm_shared (left out: APC or period, set again before it fired,
cancelled, duplicated, another thread), tm_other_thread, tm_too_old, tm_pool_full. The
export grows to 2,176 bytes; size (ULONG at byte 48, padding before) says how many bytes
the build writes: 2,176 now, 0 in d2r8. Version stays 1. On with the request counts
(WINE_D2R_CALL_COUNTS).

Model test: the pool code compiled from sync.c against a model wineserver with timer.c's
rules and esync waits runs random programs of creates (several access masks), sets
(relative, absolute, infinite, with APC, periodic, arguments that look like the marker),
waits (single, any, all), cancels, queries, duplicates and closes from two threads, where a
set request can stay on its way for 1 to 4 steps while the other thread acts. Every result
the program sees is compared with the same program under WINE_D2R_TIMER_REUSE=0: 800
programs of 20,000 steps (400 seeds, normal and tiny pool sizes), 0 differences, and a
reused timer always has the asked type and access. With the reuse number check taken out of
the server 24 of 60 seeds differ, with the unset entry dropped before the request 45 of 60,
so the test does see these races. A D2R-like loop (make, set 1 ms, wait, close): 3 counted
server calls per cycle down to 1 (the model does not count get_inproc_sync_fd).

WINE_D2R_TIMER_REUSE=0 closes anonymous timers and makes new ones as stock Wine. Read once
per process.
